  reason: "The verb's leaf producer is host Rust because its subject is a filesystem walk the hermetic floor refuses; the route around that leaf is hand-mirrored because none of the three modeled modules is in the v1 seed's emitted closure, and the two alternatives are worse -- routing through the interpreter makes it load-bearing for a NEW capability while two lanes delete it, and growing the emitter's closure to reach extdeps.bazel.label is a larger seed change than the verb it serves. What the growth buys is that the repository's instruments become addressable by label at all: before it, no target in this repository could be run by name. The diagnostic-census producer and its cli_run carriers joined under the same reasoning: the whole-tree compile-clean closure is a host effect no hermetic witness may run, and the census restores the reachability the gunbc#9160 sweep removed with the unrostered bin. The 2026-09-09 raw-census rework added the identity, disposition, refusal and specimen carriers: binding the observation to its source vector, compiler, policy, schema and closure is hashing and a total-match specimen over CompilerDiagnostic, both host facts.",
  owning_dissolution_lane: "v1-hand-queue-drain" as RoadmapNodeId,
  trigger: "Discharge each declaration when its modeled realization is emitted rather than authored. The target_invocation_host subset deletes when gunbc.target_invocation and gunbc.instrument_targets enter the emitted closure of whatever renders gunbc's CLI; the behavioral_receipt_host subset deletes when the three already-modeled behavioral producers gain emitted native realization. A PARTIAL migration is admissible and expected: label admission dissolves when extdeps.bazel.label is emitted, independently of either producer family. Moving a declaration to another host file or adding a parity witness does not discharge it -- both leave the hand-authored declaration standing.\n\nTHE floor_memory_supervisor SUBSET HAS A DIFFERENT AND HONESTLY WEAKER TRIGGER, stated separately because assimilating it to the sentence above would promise a discharge that is not in sight. Its operations are resource effects -- read /proc/self/cgroup, spawn a child, wait on it, read /sys/fs/cgroup after the child is gone -- and NONE of the four has a bound native realization. The mechanism once named here was wrong and is corrected rather than softened: it read that resource operations resolve only inside a workflow function realized by the seed INTERPRETER. There is no workflow function -- gunbc#11528 deleted the concept -- and admissibility is the `uses` clause, an effect requirement. The measured refusal `receiver type Primitive(Filesystem) establishes no method surface` off a plain fn stands; what it establishes is that a resource METHOD needs a declared requirement, not that an emitted binary cannot perform an effect. So this subset does NOT discharge by the emitter reaching gunbc.instrument_targets, and it does not discharge on the Filesystem work of demand_engine_program D11 either, because spawn and wait are outside that denominator. It discharges when the substrate can express a SUPERVISED CHILD PROCESS -- spawn, wait on a termination status, and read a filesystem path after that child is gone -- as modeled effects with typed refusals, at which point the fold replaces this file and the dispatch arm calls it. Until that capability exists the subset is frozen rather than shrinking, and the honest reading is that it is the newest and least dischargeable growth on this row. WHAT DOES NOT DISCHARGE IT: moving the judgment further into .dag (already done -- the verdict is gunbc.floor_memory_demand's and no threshold lives in the Rust), nor adding witnesses over the read, because neither removes a single one of the four effects.",
  current_boundary: "src/v1/stage0/src/target_invocation_host.rs; src/v1/stage0/src/behavioral_receipt_host.rs (native realization of the already-modeled behavioral producers, relocated from claim_executor); src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs (two #[path] mod lines plus the `floor_memory_supervisor` mod line); src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/floor_memory_supervisor.rs (the supervised cgroup read behind //gunbc/instruments:floor-memory-qualification: create nothing, spawn the measured child into the supervisor's OWN cgroup, wait, then read memory.peak and memory.events from outside the dead process. It is host Rust because every operation in it is one no .dag fold can perform -- reading /proc/self/cgroup, spawning a process, waiting on it, and reading /sys/fs/cgroup after it is gone -- and because an in-process observer cannot report the terminations that matter: a Drop guard does not run on abort, process::exit or SIGKILL, and Rust's allocation-error handler aborts, so OOM and allocation failure are exactly the cases only an outliving supervisor can report. The JUDGMENT over what it reads is NOT here: it is gunbc.floor_memory_demand qualify_floor_memory_from_readings, and this module deliberately holds no threshold, no arm and no verdict); src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/compile_clean.rs (the raw diagnostic census carriers: CensusGateDisposition, CensusSeverity, RawDiagnosticClassObservation, CompileCleanDiagnosticCensusIdentity, CompileCleanDiagnosticCensus, CompileCleanDiagnosticCensusRefusal, compile_clean_diagnostic_class_specimen, compile_clean_diagnostic_census, compile_clean_diagnostic_policy_snapshot, census_instance_disposition, compiler_diagnostic_constructor_census, census_gate_tag, census_severity_tag, census_identity_digest, diagnostic_census_refusal_rendered); generated gunbc_cli_dispatch_generated.rs (modeled Commands variant and generic retained-host selection); src/v1/stage0/src/main.rs (retained target producer host method only); dag/gunbc/target_invocation_seed_growth.dag"
}
